WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.260
"Are There Health Benefits of
Spending Time in Nature?"

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:17.019
For perhaps 99.99% of our
time on Earth as a species,

00:00:17.019 --> 00:00:21.429
we’ve been living outdoors
in the natural environment.

00:00:21.429 --> 00:00:26.150
Might there be a health benefit
to returning now and again,

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:28.509
and surrounding ourselves with nature?

00:00:28.509 --> 00:00:31.210
That’s a question urban
planners have asked:

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:33.120
Are people living in
greener areas healthier

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.180
than people living in less green areas?

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:38.730
Should we put in a park
or another carpark?

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:42.360
In a greener environment people
report fewer symptoms of illness

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:45.580
and have better perceived
general and mental health,

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:48.050
and by a considerable amount.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:51.620
Assuming the link is cause and effect,
10% more greenspace leads to

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:54.350
a decrease in the number of
symptoms that is comparable

00:00:54.350 --> 00:01:00.450
with a decrease in age by five years,
but that is a big assumption.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.287
Still, you could imagine some potential

00:01:02.287 --> 00:01:04.820
mechanisms of why it could be.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:06.580
It could mean less air pollution.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:12.100
And air pollution is no joke;
it's the fifth leading cause of death

00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:16.180
on Planet Earth, wiping out
about five million people a year.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:22.000
Though, of course, our diet kills twice
as many, as killer risk factor #1.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.750
So, it could be an antipollution effect.

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:28.800
Or maybe there’s something special
about experiencing greenspaces

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.520
beyond them just offering
more opportunities to exercise,

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:34.900
but that’s probably 
the simplest explanation:

00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:38.880
natural settings simply
promote “health-enhancing behavior

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:42.200
rather than having specific
and direct benefits for health.”

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:47.360
It’s harder to go jogging in
the park when there is no park.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:51.119
Ironically, it seems that even
when people have access to nature,

00:01:51.119 --> 00:01:53.750
they don’t necessarily
take advantage of it.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:57.150
And, even if there was a link,
instead of natural environments

00:01:57.150 --> 00:02:01.070
drawing out increased physical activity,
maybe physically active individuals

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:04.720
are just drawn to living
where there’s nature.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:09.300
But what I wanted to know is, apart
from the promotion of physical activity,

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:14.250
are there added benefits to health of
mere exposure to natural environments?

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:18.450
Now certainly, just exposure to
sunlight can treat things like

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:22.280
seasonal aﬀective disorder and
provide the sunshine vitamin—vitamin D.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:25.100
But are there any other inherent benefits?

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:31.220
You don’t know, until you put it to the test.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:34.480
Some of the studies are just silly, though.

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:37.418
At first, I thought this was
about academic achievement

00:02:37.418 --> 00:02:40.900
and vegetarianism,
but no—vegetation.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.420
They found a correlation
between non-forest vegetation

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:46.200
and graduation rates for schools.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:50.340
Maybe the ivy league edge
is all just ‘cause of the ivy...

00:02:50.340 --> 00:02:55.349
OK, but this study starts to
make things more interesting.

00:02:55.349 --> 00:03:00.069
The view through a window may
influence recovery from surgery.

00:03:00.069 --> 00:03:03.580
At this suburban hospital,
some patient rooms looked out at trees,

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:05.660
and others just to a brick wall.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:08.800
And, the “surgical patients assigned
to rooms with windows looking out

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.860
on a natural scene had shorter
postoperative hospital stays”

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:15.209
and took fewer potent
painkillers than similar patients

00:03:15.209 --> 00:03:18.990
in similar rooms but with
windows facing a brick wall.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:22.840
You can’t chalk that
up to a vitamin D effect.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:26.140
What could it be about
just looking at trees?

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:30.739
Maybe it’s the vitamin G,
just the color of green.

00:03:30.739 --> 00:03:33.250
We know how healthy
it is to eat our greens.

00:03:33.250 --> 00:03:35.580
What about just looking at them?

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.500
Researchers had people
exercise while watching a video

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.690
simulating going through a
natural-color green setting,

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:48.420
the same video in black and white,
or everything flipped to red, and…

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:52.019
no differences were noted
with the exception of the red

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.400
just making people feel angry.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:58.380
The most interesting suggested
mechanism I ran across was fractals.

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:01.471
You know how all the
branches of a tree kind of have

00:04:01.471 --> 00:04:04.780
the same shape of a tree themselves?

00:04:04.780 --> 00:04:08.220
Fractal patterns are found throughout
nature, where you can see a cascade

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:12.190
of self-similar patterns
over a range of magniﬁcations.

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:14.430
And hook people up to an
EEG, and for some reason

00:04:14.430 --> 00:04:17.650
our brain apparently seems to like them.

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:20.373
Regardless of the mechanism,
if you compile together all the

00:04:20.373 --> 00:04:24.590
controlled studies on using nature
as a health promotion intervention,

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:28.160
you tend to see mostly psychological
benefits, whereas the findings

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:31.800
related to physical outcomes
were less consistent.

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:34.080
The most common type
of study outcome was

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.440
self-reported measures of
different emotions.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:40.560
Like, what makes you feel better,
staring at a kiwifruit orchard,

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.720
or staring at a building?

00:04:43.720 --> 00:04:47.900
Awkwardly described, thanks
presumably to the language barrier,

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:53.340
as a comparison of
“synthetic versus organic stimulation.”

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:56.360
Natural settings may make
people more attentive, less sad,

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:58.780
but when it comes to
some objective measures

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:02.960
like blood pressure,
no significant effect.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:06.800
So, you know you ask people who
exercise outdoors and they say

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:10.400
they feel great, suggesting
that “green exercise”

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:14.020
activities have the capacity to
increase mood, focus, and energy

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:18.160
and within just like five minutes
of being out there in the woods.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:21.009
Yet these studies tended
not to be randomized trials.

00:05:21.009 --> 00:05:23.740
They just asked people who
already sought out nature

00:05:23.740 --> 00:05:28.020
what they thought about nature;
and so, no wonder they like it—

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:30.440
otherwise they wouldn’t be out there.

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:34.400
But hey, nature-based interventions
are low cost, often free in fact,

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.350
and non-invasive unless
you count the mosquitoes.

00:05:37.350 --> 00:05:39.928
So, if you want “a natural high”,

00:05:39.928 --> 00:05:43.210
I say go for it, whatever makes you happy.

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:46.930
Though evidently not all
green exercisers like trees.

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:51.820
Golfers just viewed them as obstacles.

